FSR v2 preprocessor terminology (from code)

- region
	- A chunk of the full document text with start and end character positions plus metadata (equipment identity/type).
	- Output format is a list like: start, end, metadata.

- boundary
	- An internal marker used to split the document into regions.
	- Built from detected equipment headers/section headers, stored as: character position + equipment type + ESN.
	- Not returned directly in output; it is used to construct regions.

- section
	- In preprocessor code: top-level numbered equipment headings (example pattern like "1 GAS TURBINE", "2 GENERATOR").
	- In metadata_processor_v2 code: TOC-based summary sections with title, start_page, end_page.

- sub-section
	- Numbered deeper headings (for example 3.1 or 3.1.1) used to identify nested Generator/Turbine content when top-level sectioning is not enough.
	- Implemented via subsection regex matches in preprocessor.

- char offsets
	- Character index positions in the full concatenated text.
	- Region start/end use these offsets; page_offsets maps each page to a start/end character span in full_text.

- page
	- A single parsed page from parsed_doc.pages (list of page texts).
	- Preprocessor loops page-by-page for detection/fallback.
	- TOC parsing in metadata_processor_v2 uses page numbers from text (human page numbers), then maps into page list indexes.

- header
	- Explicitly defined as equipment header patterns like:
		GAS TURBINE|GENERATOR|STEAM TURBINE|EXCITER (ESN | SYxxxxxxx)
	- These headers are a key source for ESN/type detection and boundary creation.

Notes on definition status
- All requested terms are represented in code behavior.
- "boundary" is internal (not a public field), but clearly implemented and used.

How everything connects (simple map)

Parsed input
- pages (list of page text)
- page_offsets (char start/end per page in full_text)
- full_text (all pages concatenated)

Flow
1. headers + sections + sub-sections are detected in full_text
2. each match becomes a boundary (char position + type + ESN)
3. sorted boundaries create region spans
	- region.start = current boundary position
	- region.end = next boundary position (or end of full_text)
4. if boundary split is weak, fallback uses page-level assignment
	- pages are tagged by signatures/forms
	- adjacent pages with same metadata are merged using page_offsets continuity
5. final output from preprocessor
	- regions (char-offset chunks with metadata)
	- metadata/hints
6. metadata_processor_v2 then adds TOC summary sections separately
	- TOC sections (title/start_page/end_page) -> extracted text
	- stored in metadata.document_summary

Relationship diagram (Mermaid)

```mermaid
flowchart TD
	A[ParsedDocument input<br/>pages + full_text + page_offsets]

	A --> B1[Header detection]
	A --> B2[Section detection]
	A --> B3[Sub-section detection]

	B1 --> C[Boundaries<br/>char position + equip type + ESN]
	B2 --> C
	B3 --> C

	C --> D{2 or more distinct equipment types?}

	D -->|Yes| E[Create region by boundary span<br/>start = current boundary pos<br/>end = next boundary pos or EOF]
	D -->|No / weak split| F[Page-level fallback assignment<br/>tag each page by forms/signatures/ESN]
	F --> G[Merge adjacent pages with same metadata<br/>using contiguous page_offsets]

	E --> H[preprocessor output<br/>regions + metadata + hints]
	G --> H

	A --> I[TOC entry extraction]
	I --> J[Build summary sections<br/>title + start_page + end_page]
	J --> K[Extract section text from pages]

	H --> L[metadata_processor_v2 final result]
	K --> L
	L --> M[metadata.document_summary added]
```

Important nuance
- There is no explicit join that maps TOC sections to specific regions.
- They coexist in the same final payload, but come from different logic paths.

Risk note: inferred section ends

Current behavior
- In preprocessor logic, section/sub-section matches are start markers.
- End is inferred later as the next boundary or end of document when regions are built.

Will this be a problem?
- Not always. It is usually fine for region-level routing and metadata attribution.
- It can become a problem when boundaries are missed or noisy.

Main risks
- Missed boundary: one section can absorb text from the next section.
- False boundary: one section can be split too early.
- Harder debugging: no standalone section span object to inspect directly.
- Format drift: irregular headings make inferred ends less stable across documents.

Suggested hardening (if needed)
- Emit explicit section spans (type/title, start_char, end_char, source).
- Keep current region output, but derive it from explicit spans.
- Track basic quality metrics: uncovered text, overlapping spans, and span-length outliers.

Boundary types (preprocessor)

Boundary sources
- Equipment header boundaries
	- Source pattern: equipment header line with (ESN | SYxxxxxxx)
	- Type comes from matched equipment label.
- Top-level numbered section boundaries
	- Source pattern: numbered equipment heading (SECTION_HDR).
	- Type is canonicalized from heading text.
- Generator sub-section boundaries
	- Source pattern: numbered subsection beginning with Generator.
	- Type is Generator.
- Turbine-related sub-section boundaries
	- Source pattern: numbered subsection beginning with Turbine/Compressor/Combustion/Inlet.
	- Type is Gas Turbine if gt_esn exists, otherwise Steam Turbine if st_esn exists.

Canonical boundary type values
- Gas Turbine
- Generator
- Steam Turbine
- Exciter

Filtering rule
- Boundaries linked to inactive ESNs are dropped before region creation.
